}

^["recursive_improvement"]{
 loop: [
  "? reread current configs, scripts, and release surfaces before adding more measurements",
  "< ship one repeatable experiment or output form at a time",
  "$ record what became faster, clearer, or more discussable",
  "^ project surviving results into docs, .spw, or release QA rather than leaving them in logs",
  "% keep one stable baseline while experimenting with versions or settings",
 ]
 gates: [#baseline_stability, #output_legibility, #release_truth, #claim_discipline]
}

^["parallel_work"]{
 critical_path: [#measurement_protocol, #stable_baseline]
 parallelizable: [#compiler_profiles, #version_matrix, #projection_outputs, #bundle_qa]
 shared_hot_files: ["package.json", "tsconfig.json", "tsconfig.typecheck.json", "scripts/release/build-js-dist.ts", "docs/research/index.spw"]
 merge_risks: [#script_name_drift, #output_schema_drift, #release_claim_drift]
 handoff_artifacts: [@plan, @artifact, @package, @solution]
}

^["supporting_documents"]{
 must_ship: [@plan, @artifact, @research_index]
 helpful: [@runtime_index, @ts_extended_diagnostics, @ts_generate_trace, @ts_native_preview, @ts7_progress]
 carries: [#benchmark_table, #trace_notes, #version_matrix, #bundle_notes, #release_qa, #interpretation_guide]
}

^["interaction_depth"]{
 subjects: [#comparison, #legibility, #reversibility, #latency, #trust, #surprise]
 questions: [#what_counts_as_faster, #what_changes_editor_feel, #what_impacts_release, #what_is_projection_worthy]
 qualities: [#clarity, #containment, #traceability]
}

^["stream"]{
 >> [2026-03-28 14:34] observe — the repo now has a referenced tsconfig graph, but there is no local observatory that can explain whether those changes improved indexing, editor feel, or release surfaces.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:35] observe — current scripts cover build, jsdist, pack dry-run, and several audits, but there is no explicit TypeScript profile/version matrix or projection-friendly reporting path.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:36] observe — the user wants curiosity-friendly testing, not just a one-time optimization: configuration settings, TypeScript 7 comparison, output forms, bundle inspection, and release implication QA should be inspectable and communicable.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:37] decide — this lane should remain local-first and optional: TS7/native preview can be tested, but it should not silently become a default or release gate.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:38] decide — the value surface is not only "faster compile"; it is also explainability. Measurements should be serializable into JSON, Markdown, and .spw so they can feed docs, research notes, and release claims.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:39] taste — the strongest version of this work turns toolchain folklore into reusable engineering taste: what changed, what stayed invariant, and which surfaces were actually affected.
 >> [2026-07-20] observe — TypeScript 7.0 is stable (native Go); TypeScript 6.0 is the bridge. Repo still on ~5.9.3; no typescript-profile scripts landed yet.
 >> [2026-07-20] decide — split execution: typescript-perf-audit-infra owns first profiler/matrix/package scripts; typescript-upgrade-ladder owns 5.9→6→7 dual-install; this plan retains research projections, bundle QA narrative, and broader comparison axes.
 >> [2026-07-20] revise — version-matrix language should treat TS7 as installable stable, not only native-preview; dual-install with @typescript/typescript6 remains required for eslint API until 7.1.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:38] observe — reconciled against main: ~[1] landed in b41caa6b (plan staged); no TypeScript profile or matrix script exists under scripts/analyzers and no docs/research/spw/typescript-toolchain-observatory.spw exists, so ~[2]..~[7] are open. By the 2026-07-20 split decision, ~[2] and ~[3] are the same work as typescript-perf-audit-infra ~[2] and ~[3] (single-run profiler, version and config matrix), so they are moved there rather than duplicated here.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:39] revise — ?[preview_install] resolved by the 2026-07-20 split (TS 7 is installable stable and typescript-upgrade-ladder rung C owns the dual-install, so the observatory consumes it) and moved to ^[done]. ?[projection_schema] and ?[release_gate] sharpened against the perf-audit identity row and the existing build:jsdist and pack:jsdist:dry scripts; ?[editor_protocol] sharpened to the decision it unblocks. ~[1] moved to ^[done].
}

^["open"]{
 ?[editor_protocol]: "Can editor-indexing time be measured by a local script (for example tsserver logs) or only by a manual VS Code protocol? Decides whether ~[6] publishes a script or a checklist for that axis."
 ?[projection_schema]: "Is the perf-audit identity row (compiler, config, duration, pass) enough to feed JSON, Markdown, and .spw projections, or does the observatory need extra fields such as bundle size and declaration output? Decides the row contract that ~[4] reads."
 ?[release_gate]: "Should the build:jsdist and pack:jsdist:dry results (file list and size) be recorded as observatory rows per toolchain variant, or stay a manual release step? Decides whether ~[5] adds a recorded row or only a narrative."
}

